.TH DANBY-METHODS 1
.SH NAME
danby-methods \- compare Euler, Heun, RK4, and adaptive RKF4(5)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B temple/methods
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Methods
is an interactive numerical-methods laboratory with three initial-value
problems:
.EX
y' = y,              y(0) = 1
y' = -y,             y(0) = 1
y' = 1.8y(1-y),      y(0) = 0.08.
.EE
They have analytic exponential or logistic solutions.  The background vectors
form a direction field: at every displayed state they show the local direction
specified by the differential equation, independently of any selected initial
condition.  The upper view overlays the analytic solution with explicit Euler, improved Euler
(Heun), classical fourth-order Runge-Kutta, and the accepted points selected
by adaptive Fehlberg RKF4(5).
.PP
Each orange Euler arrow is the literal construction
.IR (t,y)+h(1,f(t,y)) :
the method follows the direction at the beginning of the interval for one
whole step.  Comparing those arrows with the direction field explains the
method's accumulated error geometrically.
.PP
The lower view plots endpoint error against step size on logarithmic
coordinates.  The slopes approach one for Euler, two for Heun, and four for
RK4.  This provides numerical confirmation of method order rather than merely
showing that smaller steps look smoother.
.PP
RKF4(5) uses absolute and relative error tolerances to accept or reject each
trial step.  Its accepted and rejected counts are displayed separately from
the fixed-step control.
.SH CONTROLS
Drag the bottom logarithmic track to vary the fixed step from 0.005 to 0.8.
The
.B +
and
.B -
keys decrease and increase it.
.B 1 ,
.B 2 ,
and
.B 3
select exponential growth, exponential decay, and nonlinear logistic growth.
.B D
switches the lower view between convergence order and adaptive diagnostics.
The diagnostic view shows the logarithms of trial step size and normalized
error against trial time; orange points are rejected attempts.  A normalized
error at or below zero on this logarithmic scale is acceptable.
.B R
restores the initial setting, and
.B Q
or Escape exits.
.SH INTERPRETATION
Euler follows the tangent at the beginning of each interval and therefore
systematically underestimates this convex solution.  Heun averages the first
slope and a predicted endpoint slope.  RK4 combines four slopes.  The lower
plot measures the resulting endpoint errors at a common final time, avoiding
a misleading comparison based only on curve appearance.
